          minWords: 250,
          model: "Nowadays, more and more students study online instead of attending classes at school or university. Although this trend has some advantages, I believe that its disadvantages are bigger, especially for young students.\n\nOn the one hand, online courses have some clear advantages. First, they are flexible, so students can study at any time and in any place. They can also watch a lesson again if they do not understand it, which is very useful for people who have part-time jobs or family responsibilities. Second, online courses are often cheaper than normal classes. For example, a student who lives in the countryside can take a course from a famous university without paying for travel or a room in the city.\n\nOn the other hand, studying online has some serious drawbacks. The biggest problem is the lack of face-to-face communication with teachers and classmates. In a classroom, students can ask questions at once and share ideas with their classmates, but online students often feel lonely. In addition, studying at home is not easy because there are many distractions, such as smartphones and games. As a result, some students stop studying and do not finish the course. Teachers also cannot easily check whether students are really working. Moreover, some subjects, such as science experiments and sport, are very difficult to learn through a screen.\n\nIn conclusion, although online courses are flexible and cheap, I believe that these advantages are outweighed by the disadvantages, because students lose contact with other people and need strong self-discipline. Therefore, I think online learning should be used to support normal classes, not to replace them, especially for teenagers.",
